Subject: [PATCH 09077/11362] Escalate recurring blockers: one message blocked
 31 tasks across 14 milestones unnoticed

REQ-OPS-RECURRING-BLOCKER-6263, answering "how do we make the unattended
operation more self improving?"

Unattended operation fails differently from attended operation: a failure nobody
reads never gets fixed, so it recurs indefinitely and the loop keeps paying for
it. Measured over the last 14 milestones: 58 blocked tasks, and 31 of them carry
the IDENTICAL verdict blocked_gate_check_failed -- one blocker, 31 times, spanning
.536 to .549, with nothing escalating it. Blocked is already the largest category
of all work (31% of 108 tasks). The same shape appeared in the publish path the
same day: a stale marker aborted every dataset publish for six days while blaming
the wrong cause. Attended, someone investigates on day one.

The ledger groups blocked verdicts by NORMALISED message, stripping experiment
ids, milestone versions and embedded counts. That is the load-bearing part: without
it every blocker looks like a singleton and the recurrence is invisible. Tests pin
both directions -- ids must not hide a recurrence, and genuinely different blockers
must not merge.

--emit-known-issue appends one dated MANDATORY-NEXT-MILESTONE entry, reusing the
Overdue-Priority Forcing Function rather than adding a new enforcement path. It
appends rather than rewrites, and writes one entry for all blockers so a
mechanical process cannot flood the priorities list. Run once here; the x31
blocker is now scheduled for investigation.

It does not block anything. A recurring blocker may be correct -- a gate refusing
genuinely unready work is doing its job. What is unacceptable is that nothing
looked.

SECOND FINDING. 37 of 58 blocked artifacts record NO reason at all, and all 31
blocked_gate_check_failed artifacts carry no gate_reason or equivalent. A blocked
verdict with no diagnostic cannot be investigated without re-running the task,
which is precisely how the same blocker recurs indefinitely. That argues for
making a diagnostic field mandatory on blocked verdicts -- the same
convention-over-tooling conclusion the row-consistency work reached.

